'''Joseph Ferdinand Gould''' (12 September 1889 – 18 August 1957)<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=MN1g-W8T7nAC&pg=PR15 Raymond J Rundus, ''Joseph Mitchell: A Reader's and Writer's Guide'']. Retrieved 8 February 2014</ref> was an American eccentric, also known as '''Professor Seagull<!--redirect-->'''. Often homeless, he claimed to be the author of the longest book ever written, ''An Oral History of the Contemporary World'', also known as ''An Oral History of Our Time'' or ''Meo Tempore''. He inspired the book ''[[Joe Gould's Secret]]'' (1965) by [[Joseph Mitchell (writer)|Joseph Mitchell]], and [[Joe Gould's Secret (film)|its film adaptation]] (2000), and is a character in the 2009 computer game ''[[The Blackwell Convergence]]''.

{{Short description|Study of retail stores placement}}
[[File:Pattern of Shopping Preferences Regional - Appliances, Clothing, Car Sales & Furniture(1972).jpg|thumb|Map of shopping preferences in Manitoba in 1972]]
{{Geography sidebar}}
'''Retail geography''' is the study of where to place retail stores based on where their customers are. The use of retail geography has grown significantly in the 2000s as a result of the use of [[geographic information systems]] ([[GIS]]). It first emerged in the [[United States]] in the 1960s.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Wrigley |first1=N. |title=International Encyclopedia of Human Geography |chapter=Retail Geographies |date=2009 |pages=398–405 |doi=10.1016/B978-008044910-4.00226-1 |isbn=978-0-08-044910-4 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Barnes |first1=T.J. |title=International Encyclopedia of Human Geography |chapter=Economic Geography |date=2009 |pages=315–327 |doi=10.1016/B978-008044910-4.00150-4 |isbn=978-0-08-044910-4 }}</ref> In the 1990s, the "new retail geography" emerged.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Wrigley |first1=Neil |title=International Encyclopedia of Human Geography |chapter=Retail Geographies |date=2009 |pages=479–485 |doi=10.1016/B978-0-08-102295-5.10216-1 |isbn=978-0-08-102296-2 }}</ref>

{{Short description|Astronomy according to different cultures}}
'''Cultural astronomy''', sometimes called the study of '''Astronomy in Culture''', has been described as investigating "the diversity of ways in which cultures, both ancient and modern, perceive celestial objects and integrate them into their view of the world."<ref> {{ Citation | last1 = Ruggles | first1 = Clive L. N. | author-link = Clive L. N. Ruggles | last2 = Saunders | first2 = Nicholas J. | author2-link = Nicholas J. Saunders | editor-last = Ruggles | editor-first = Clive L. N. | editor-link = Clive L. N. Ruggles | editor2-last = Saunders | editor2-first = Nicholas J. | editor2-link = Nicholas J. Saunders | date = 1993 | title = Astronomies and Cultures | chapter = The Study of Cultural Astronomy | publisher = University Press of Colorado | place = Niwot, CO | pages = 1–31 | isbn = 0-87081-319-6 }}</ref> As such, it encompasses the [[interdisciplinary]] fields studying the [[astronomy|astronomies]] of current or ancient societies and cultures.<ref>{{ Citation | editor-last = Holbrook | editor-first = Jarita | editor-link = Jarita Holbrook | editor2-last = Urama | editor2-first = Johnson O. | editor3-last = Medupe | editor3-first = R. Thebe | series = Astrophysics and Space Science Proceedings |year=2008 |title=African Cultural Astronomy |volume=6 |doi = 10.1007/978-1-4020-6639-9 |bibcode = 2008ASSP....6.....H |isbn=978-1-4020-6638-2 |url=https://cds.cern.ch/record/1339110  | last1 = Holbrook | first1 = Jarita C. | last2 = Urama | first2 = Johnson O. | last3 = Medupe | first3 = R. Thebe | url-access = subscription }}</ref> It developed from the two interdisciplinary fields of [[archaeoastronomy]], the study of the use of astronomy and its role in ancient cultures and civilizations, and [[ethnoastronomy]], "a closely allied research field which merges astronomy, textual scholarship, ethnology, and the interpretation of ancient iconography for the purpose of reconstructing lifeways, astronomical techniques, and rituals."<ref>{{ Citation | last = Baity | first = Elizabeth Chesley | date = 1973 | title = Archaeoastronomy and Ethnoastronomy So Far | journal = Current Anthropology | volume = 14 | issue = 4 | page = 390 | doi = 10.1086/201351 | jstor = 2740842 }}</ref> Cultural astronomy is also related to [[historical astronomy]] (analyzing historical astronomical data), [[history of astronomy]] (understanding and study and evolution of the discipline of astronomy over the course of human knowledge) and [[history of astrology]] (investigating relationships between [[astrology and astronomy]]).{{Cn|date=July 2025}}

Research on cultural astronomy may be published in the ''Journal of Astronomy in Culture'', which was established in 2016 by the International Society for Archaeoastronomy and Astronomy in Culture (ISAAC).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Journal of Astronomy in Culture: About Us |url=https://escholarship.org/uc/jac/aboutus |access-date=2 July 2025 |website=Journal of Astronomy in Culture}}</ref>

{{Short description|Hierarchical level in biological classification}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=September 2020}}

[[File:Taxonomic Rank Graph.svg|right|thumb|upright=1.7|The major ranks: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, and species, applied to the [[red fox]], ''Vulpes vulpes''. Many classifications based on [[genetic analysis]] require more than these eight ranks, not all of which have distinct names.]]

In [[taxonomy (biology)|biological taxonomy]], a '''taxonomic rank''' denotes the level that a group of organisms{{em dash}}either taxon or clade{{em dash}}occupies in a hierarchical system of classification, which is usually but not necessarily based on evolutionary relationships between those organisms. Some authors prefer to use the term '''nomenclatural rank''',<ref name="Dubois 2007a">{{cite journal |last1=Dubois |first1=Alain |title=Phylogeny, taxonomy and nomenclature: the problem of taxonomic categories and of nomenclatural ranks |journal=Zootaxa |date=2007 |volume=1519 |pages=27–68 |doi=10.11646/zootaxa.1519.1.3 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/279549116}}</ref> contending that, according to some definitions, the hierarchical rank occupied by a group of organisms is more accurately described as concept of [[Binomial nomenclature#Relationship to classification and taxonomy|nomenclature]] rather than [[Taxonomy (biology)#Definition|taxonomy]]. In general, the most inclusive taxa (or clades), such as the [[Eukaryote|Eukarya]] and [[Animal]]ia, are assigned the highest ranks in a particular system of taxonomy, whereas the least inclusive ones, such as ''[[Homo sapiens]]'', ''[[Bufo bufo]]'', ''[[Tyrannosaurus rex]]'', and ''[[Vulpes vulpes]]'', are given the lowest ranks.

Ranks can be either "absolute", in which a pre-defined set of several descriptive terms (such as [[species]], [[genus]], [[Tribe (taxonomy)|tribe]], [[Family (taxonomy)|family]], [[Order (taxonomy)|order]], [[Class (taxonomy)|class]], [[phylum]], [[Kingdom (taxonomy)|kingdom]], and [[Domain (taxonomy)|domain]]) constitutes the set of allowable ranks for taxa; or "relative", where ranks are designated instead by an ''indented'' taxonomy in which the level of indentation reflects the rank. In a relative system, the rank of a taxon is not necessarily an inherent attribute of the taxon so much as an attribute of a particular indented/nested hierarchy defining the relations between different groups. Additionally, relative systems of taxonomic rank often allow indefinitely adding further nested taxa to allow finer distinctions to be captured as needed.

Rank-based codes (such as the [[International Code of Zoological Nomenclature|Zoological Code]], the [[International Code of Nomenclature for algae, fungi, and plants|Botanical Code]], the [[International Code of Nomenclature for Cultivated Plants|Code for Cultivated Plants]], the [[International Code of Nomenclature of Prokaryotes|Prokaryotic Code]], and the [[Virus classification#ICTV classification|Code for Viruses]]) all require absolute ranks, but absolute ranks are not required in all [[nomenclature|nomenclatural systems]]{{em dash}}the [[PhyloCode]] (of [[phylogenetic nomenclature]]), for instance, does not require absolute ranks.<ref name="Cantino & de Queiroz 2020">{{cite book |last1=Cantino |first1=Philip D. |last2=de Queiroz |first2=Kevin |title=PhyloCode |date=2020 |publisher=CRC Press |location=Boca Raton, Fl |isbn=978-0429821356 |url=http://phylonames.org/code/ |language=en}}</ref>

