'''Macedonian literature''' ({{langx|mk|македонска книжевност}}) begins with the [[Ohrid Literary School]] in the [[First Bulgarian Empire]] (nowadays [[North Macedonia]]){{citation needed|date=November 2022}} in 886. These first written works in the dialects of the [[Old Church Slavonic]] were religious.<ref>[http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/354306/Macedonian-literature Macedonian literature] – Britannica</ref> The school was established by St. [[Clement of Ohrid]].<ref>[http://www.macedonia.co.uk/client/index1.aspx?page=201 Medieval Macedonian literature] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110927163315/http://www.macedonia.co.uk/client/index1.aspx?page=201 |date=27 September 2011 }}.</ref><ref>[http://www.cybermacedonia.com/mlanguag.html ...He introduced the language into the official sermons and prayers in Macedonia and Southern Albania, erected several monastery's and churches, delivered sermons among the people in their own language, becoming one of the first creators of Slav and Macedonian literature.] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110218143406/http://cybermacedonia.com/mlanguag.html |date=2011-02-18 }}, Macedonian language on cybermacedonia.com</ref>  The Macedonian [[recension]]{{clarify|date=November 2022}} at that time was part of the [[Old Church Slavonic]] and it did not represent one regional dialect but a generalized form of early [[Eastern South Slavic]].<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=7BXJgfIo_fYC&q=Old+Church+Slavonic+Grammar+Horace+Lunt Old Church Slavonic grammar, Horace Gray Lunt, Walter de Gruyter, 2001, p. 1.], {{ISBN|3-11-016284-9}}</ref> The standardization of Macedonian in the 20th century provided good ground for further development of the modern Macedonian literature and this period is the richest one in the history of the literature itself.

{{Short description|Literary genre supporting feminist goals}}
[[File:Butler signing.jpg|thumb|[[Octavia Butler]], award-winning [[feminist science fiction]] author]]
{{Feminism sidebar}}
'''Feminist literature''' is fiction, nonfiction, drama, or poetry, which supports the [[Feminism|feminist]] goals of defining, establishing, and defending equal [[Civil and political rights|civil, political, economic]], and [[social rights]] for women. It often addresses the roles of women in society particularly as regarding status, privilege, and power – and generally portrays the consequences to women, men, families, communities, and societies as undesirable.

{{short description|Arts originating from New Zealand}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=July 2023}}
[[Image:Hinepare.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Portrait of Hinepare of [[Ngāti Kahungunu]] (1890) by [[Gottfried Lindauer]], showing [[tā moko|chin moko]], [[pounamu]] [[hei-tiki]] and woven cloak]]
'''New Zealand art''' consists of the [[visual arts|visual]] and [[plastic arts]] (including woodwork, textiles, and ceramics) originating from [[New Zealand]] and comes from different traditions: indigenous [[Māori art]] and that brought here including from early British and sometimes Irish settlers.

Visual artwork as defined in New Zealand includes paintings, drawings, carvings, printing such as lithographs and woodcuts, and prints (including books of prints). It also includes photographs, sculptures, collages, models and works of art in the form of crafts, ceramics, glassware, jewellery, textiles, weaving, metalware, furniture, and computer and electronic art, from European, Māori and Pacific artists.<ref>{{Cite journal |date=12 April 2023 |title=Resale Right for Visual Artists Regulations : Discussion Document 3 |url=https://mch.govt.nz/sites/default/files/ARRS-consultation-doc-20230412-4.pdf |journal=Manatū Taonga Ministry for Culture and Heritage}}</ref>

