'''Astrobotic Technology, Inc.''', commonly referred to as '''Astrobotic''',<ref>{{Cite web |last=Foust |first=Jeff |date=2024-01-18 |title=Astrobotic confirms Peregrine reentry plans |url=https://spacenews.com/astrobotic-confirms-peregrine-reentry-plans/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240226120522/https://spacenews.com/astrobotic-confirms-peregrine-reentry-plans/ |archive-date=February 26, 2024 |access-date=2024-01-19 |website=SpaceNews |language=en-US}}</ref> is an American [[private company]] that is developing [[space robotics]] technology for lunar and planetary missions. It was founded in 2007 by [[Carnegie Mellon University|Carnegie Mellon]] professor [[Red Whittaker]] and his associates with the goal of winning the [[Google Lunar X Prize]].<ref>{{cite press release|last=Krovel|first=Trond|url=http://www.lunarexplorers.net/node/386|title=Astrobotic Technology and Raytheon Collaborate to Pursue Google Lunar X Prize|work=Astrobotic Technology|publisher=Lunar Explorers Society|date=December 10, 2007|access-date=February 14, 2008|archive-date=November 21, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091121165436/http://www.lunarexplorers.net/node/386|url-status=live}}</ref> The company is based in [[Pittsburgh]], Pennsylvania. Their first launch occurred on January 8, 2024,<ref name="NASA-20240107">{{Cite web |title=NASA Sets Coverage for ULA, Astrobotic Artemis Robotic Moon Launch - NASA |url=https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-sets-coverage-for-ula-astrobotic-artemis-robotic-moon-launch/ |access-date=2024-01-07 |language=en-US |archive-date=January 7, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240107054342/https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-sets-coverage-for-ula-astrobotic-artemis-robotic-moon-launch/ |url-status=live }}</ref> as part of NASA's [[Commercial Lunar Payload Services]] (CLPS) program. The launch carried the company's [[Peregrine lunar lander|''Peregrine'' lunar lander]] on board the first flight of the [[Vulcan Centaur]] rocket from Florida's [[Cape Canaveral Space Force Station|Space Force Station]] LC-41.<ref name="NYT-20240107">{{cite news |last=Chang |first=Kenneth |title=Vulcan Rocket's Inaugural Launch Carries Moon Lander to Space - Here's what you need to know about United Launch Alliance and the robotic lunar spacecraft it is sending to orbit. |work=The New York Times |date=January 7, 2024 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/07/science/nasa-vulcan-moon-launch.html |url-status=live |archiveurl=https://archive.today/20240107180110/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/07/science/nasa-vulcan-moon-launch.html |archivedate=7 January 2024 |accessdate=8 January 2024 }}</ref> The mission was unable to reach the Moon for a soft or hard landing.<ref name="PhysOrg-20240114">{{cite news |author=<!--not stated--> |date=14 January 2024 |title=Doomed US lunar lander now headed for Earth: company |url=https://phys.org/news/2024-01-doomed-lunar-lander-earth-company.html |work=Phys.Org |location=United Kingdom |agency=Agence France-Presse |access-date=15 January 2024 |archive-date=January 15, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240115130050/https://phys.org/news/2024-01-doomed-lunar-lander-earth-company.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
On June 11, 2020, Astrobotic received a second contract for the CLPS program. NASA would pay Astrobotic US$199.5 million to take the [[VIPER (rover)|VIPER]] rover to the Moon, targeting a landing in November 2024.<ref name="nasa-20220718">{{cite web |url=https://www.nasa.gov/feature/nasa-replans-clps-delivery-of-viper-to-2024-to-reduce-risk |title=NASA Replans CLPS Delivery of VIPER to 2024 to Reduce Risk |work=[[NASA]] |date=July 18, 2022 |access-date=July 18, 2022 |archive-date=July 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220718200943/https://www.nasa.gov/feature/nasa-replans-clps-delivery-of-viper-to-2024-to-reduce-risk/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In July 2024, NASA announced that VIPER had been cancelled.<ref name="nasa-202407172">{{Cite web |title=NASA Ends VIPER Project, Continues Moon Exploration - NASA |url=https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-ends-viper-project-continues-moon-exploration/ |access-date=2024-07-17 |language=en-US}}</ref>

